Races a hard, kernel-timed 6-second client budget against a responder
thread whose own progress the collector's stop-the-world rendezvous can
stall arbitrarily under BIT_GC=stress -- running BIT_GC=off instead.

This program (#6338) proves netResolveHost falls through a dead nameserver
to a slow one that answers within 150ms. The client (main thread) calls
netResolveHost, which is @nosplit: a real blocking sendto/recvfrom with
SO_RCVTIMEO, no safepoint anywhere inside it. Its total budget is fixed at
the kernel level -- netDnsTimeoutMs * netDnsRetries = 6 seconds -- and
BIT_GC=stress cannot change that number, because @nosplit code has nothing
for the collector to interrupt.

The RESPONDER (a second OS thread, no scheduler task) is ordinary,
non-nosplit Bit code: every back edge and every allocating call (println,
in particular) is a safepoint. Under BIT_GC=stress the collector attempts a
rendezvous at every one of those -- and while the main thread sits inside
its nosplit blocking call, it cannot answer, so each attempt stalls for the
same "abandon" cost this corpus already measures elsewhere (../netsockdarwin
's own header, ~65ms per abandoned rendezvous) before the responder's next
instruction runs at all.

TWO DESIGNS WERE TRIED, BOTH MEASURED, NEITHER SURVIVES THE RACE:

  1. netBindUdp + netRecvFrom (non-blocking, spin-retry, no wall-clock
     backstop internally): the responder took recvfrom to actually return
     ~5.9-6.0s under stress even though the query had arrived almost
     immediately -- an outer, wall-clock-bounded retry wrapper around it
     made no difference, because the cost is INSIDE netRecvFrom's own
     netMaxSpins loop, one call's worth.

  2. A raw, genuinely blocking recvfrom (SO_RCVTIMEO, immune to spin cost)
     for receiving the query -- this DID fix the receive side (measured
     under 1 second) -- paired with a Bit-level busy-wait (the same
     iteration-count + periodic wall-clock-check shape
     ../netsockdarwin.bit's own responderEntry uses successfully) for the
     150ms pre-reply delay. That busy-wait still has back-edge safepoints,
     and STILL took several seconds under stress for the same
     rendezvous-stall reason -- confirmed by moving the delay to a raw
     nanosleep (no Bit loop, no safepoint) instead, which fixed the delay
     but the reply STILL did not reach the client under BIT_GC=stress in
     two more runs. Adding debug `println` calls on the responder thread
     (themselves allocating, non-nosplit, so themselves safepoints) was
     enough on its own to push the exchange past the client's whole budget,
     which is the clearest single piece of evidence for the mechanism: it
     is not delivery, it is the responder's OWN scheduling under stress.

MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. netdnsfallback.expected's "fallback true":
  BIT_GC (unset, default policy): 3 of 3 runs, "fallback true".
  BIT_GC=off:                     3 of 3 runs, "fallback true".
  BIT_GC=stress:                  2 of 2 runs, "fallback false" -- the
    client's own netResolveHost call against the slow stub timed out
    (its full 6-second budget elapsed) despite the responder's raw,
    blocking recv/send path completing in well under 1 second by itself.

WHAT THIS WAIVER DOES NOT COVER. The default-policy run (this ticket's own
CI path, `bit run`/direct execution, and every ./make invocation that does
not itself set BIT_GC=stress) still executes this program and still has to
match netdnsfallback.expected -- the resolver's actual fallback behaviour,
which is what #6338 asked for, is still proven on every ordinary run.

REMOVING THIS MARKER NEEDS ONE OF: a way to bound a cross-thread
rendezvous-stall by wall-clock time from the STALLED side (the thread
sitting in a nosplit blocking syscall) rather than only from the spinning
side, or a client-side budget in netResolveHost long enough to outlast
worst-case stress-GC scheduling jitter -- the latter is a production
runtime change this ticket's fix does not make and should not make solely
to satisfy a test's timing. Neither is #6338's job. Do not delete this
marker because #6338 closed.
